{"title":"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 20","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A triangular prism is placed on a rectangular prism, as shown. The volume of the combined structure, in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/8/5b86b317546185ec08e0585ad02dd467c78abc0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}^{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" /> is.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/8/e687491f9defd6a714546596e2d7111b8882f1bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 76 \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 78 \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 72 \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 84 \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 66$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"433\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_20&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To find the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Volume\" title=\"Volume\">volume</a> of the combined structure, we can add the volume of the rectangular prism to the volume of the triangular prism.\n</p><p>The volume of a rectangular prism is equal to the product of its length, width, and height, which would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/7/2471f32c05d676888c7b5a9fb009923f21c6247a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 \\times 4 \\times 2 = 6 \\times 8 = 48$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"181\" height=\"15\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/8/5b86b317546185ec08e0585ad02dd467c78abc0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}^{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Since the height of the rectangular prism is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/759ed967199e05a556aba1e4b227b37095ae3ec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}$\" width=\"21\" height=\"7\" /> and the height of the whole structure is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/759ed967199e05a556aba1e4b227b37095ae3ec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}$\" width=\"21\" height=\"7\" />, the perpendicular height of the rectangular prism is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/759ed967199e05a556aba1e4b227b37095ae3ec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}$\" width=\"21\" height=\"7\" />.\n</p><p>Edges opposite of each other are equal in prisms, so the length of the triangular prism is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/759ed967199e05a556aba1e4b227b37095ae3ec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}$\" width=\"21\" height=\"7\" /> and the base is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/3/3b3229ae4ae14dc245b5cb2e44aefff628f2ce60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6 - 3 = 3$\" width=\"73\" height=\"12\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/759ed967199e05a556aba1e4b227b37095ae3ec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}$\" width=\"21\" height=\"7\" />\n</p><p>The volume of a prism in general is equal to the product of the area of its face multiplied by its length. The area of the triangular face would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/2/93232f76992d7816b74dc27eefe5ef10ff168c27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3 \\times 3}{2} = \\frac{9}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"38\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/e/cce95ec3eb5f2ff5452281e5b03c39757b163de2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" />, and the length is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/9/759ed967199e05a556aba1e4b227b37095ae3ec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}$\" width=\"21\" height=\"7\" />, so the volume of the triangular prism would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/d/ffd7e088e3a365e13a2f56650b57157e23fa5f18.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2} \\times 4 = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"89\" height=\"38\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/8/5b86b317546185ec08e0585ad02dd467c78abc0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}^{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>Thus, the combined volume in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/8/5b86b317546185ec08e0585ad02dd467c78abc0a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{cm}^{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"17\" /> would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/0/000cba817927ed9990adfd703990111696f36f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$48 + 18 = \\boxed{\\textbf {(E) } 66}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"148\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)\">2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 19\">Problem 19</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_21\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 21\">Problem 21</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_7)_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2011 CEMC Gauss (Grade 7) Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.ph